1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:06,200
Hi, my name is Shreyom Paul, and this is the video for my app, OrcaSafe.

2
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:11,500
I'm here right now on a ferry in the Puget Sound, surrounded by the exact problem my app is trying to solve.

3
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:17,200
The heavy engine vibrations and underwater drone that you can feel through this video is a wall of noise these ships make

4
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:21,800
that physically blind our critically endangered Southern Resident killer whales.

5
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:28,800
The core problem I am tackling is simple: commercial vessel noise is drowning out an endangered orca population.

6
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:36,000
Since peaking at 98 individuals in 1995, the Southern Resident killer whales have dropped 24% to just 74 today,

7
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:40,000
driven by scarce prey, pollution, and of course, acoustic masking.

8
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:47,000
As you can see here, busy commercial shipping lanes cut directly through the whales' federally designated critical habitat.

9
00:00:47,800 --> 00:00:53,500
And every one-decibel rise in ship noise cuts an orca's odds of catching prey by 12.5%,

10
00:00:53,500 --> 00:00:59,800
muffling echolocation, weakening pod coordination, and driving whales away from prime feeding grounds.

11
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:06,500
Today's slowdown programs are voluntary and static, costing ships time even when no whales are around.

12
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:13,000
OrcaSafe acts like a dynamic school-zone light instead, using real-time AI on live hydrophone audio

13
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:17,500
to alert commercial vessel pilots to slow down only when orcas are actually detected.

14
00:01:17,800 --> 00:01:27,000
And slowing down is highly effective: dropping ship speed from 18 to 11 knots can reduce underwater acoustic noise emitted by roughly 90%.

15
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:37,000
My five-phase training pipeline pulls raw Orcasound S3 audio, slices it into 5-second chunks, turns them into mel spectrograms,

16
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:44,500
and then trains a frozen MobileNetV2 classifier head on it—achieving 92.6% accuracy and 99.6% precision.

17
00:01:44,800 --> 00:01:54,500
This is my custom-engineered PyTorch transfer learning pipeline, training my MobileNetV2 model to recognize distinct orca vocalizations with over 92% accuracy.

18
00:01:55,200 --> 00:02:02,000
At runtime, every 10-second audio polling fetches live S3 audio chunks, runs DSP and inference via FastAPI on Cloud Run,

19
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:07,500
and returns a single JSON payload to the web client.

20
00:02:08,200 --> 00:02:18,000
Ultimately, my model runs inside one lightweight Cloud Run container, linking live hydrophone streams and vessel telemetry directly to an interactive web alert map.

21
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:22,200
Now let's go to a demo of my app in action.

22
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:28,000
This is OrcaSafe, running live on the cloud. Every blue dot here is a hydrophone in the Salish Sea.

23
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:34,500
You can tap any node to see what it's hearing, like here at Sunset Bay, you can hear the live audio and see the spectrogram.

24
00:02:34,500 --> 00:02:40,000
Every 10 seconds this audio is being scored. It's at below 85% right now, so it stays clear.

25
00:02:40,300 --> 00:02:47,000
Now here, the map is lit up: Bush Point, 95% detection. An orca is here, so a slowdown alert's issued.

26
00:02:47,800 --> 00:02:57,500
By connecting real-time acoustic detection with GPS, OrcaSafe ensures that our global commerce and marine life can share the same Sound.